+
+### 4.1 The command table
+
+The command layer is one engine over a reviewed data file per target,
+`targets/<id>/commands.toml` (loaded by `nl_shell.gate.commands`). Wrappers, the sudo
+family, destructive commands, interpreters and builtins are rows; no command has a code
+path of its own. The table is reviewed like the allowlist (§6, Versioning): a row gives a
+command its meaning, so removing a row, an option or an effect can lower a class and is a
+v2 change. The gate MUST read the table only from the fixed location of the active target:
+no path argument, environment variable, working directory or config file can change it.
+
+Each row gives the command words it covers, a minimal example, its own class (at least
+`mutating`), and its argv grammar: options with and without values, in short, attached
+and long `=` forms (with unambiguous long-option abbreviations); `--`; where an inner
+command starts; and what every field does. A field is one of: a plain argument, an inner
+command (*runs-command-at*), text in a named grammar (*runs-text*), a script file
+(*runs-file-as-code*), stdin read as code (*runs-stdin-as-code*), code kept to run later
+(*stores-code*), a variable name (*sets-variable*), or a descriptor. A row can also
+elevate (*elevates*), destroy under a condition (*destroys*), reject a stdin feed, open an
+interactive shell (*opens-shell*) or read code from the terminal (*reads-terminal*). A
+row whose class does not depend on argv (a setuid binary that does not elevate, such as
+`passwd`) MAY say so and give a fixed class.
+
+Three decisions:
+
+- **A.** A command with no row (and no allowlist entry) is at least `mutating`, "not
+  allowlisted". If any of its argument words, after quote removal, names a row that
+  elevates, the command is `privileged` and a proposal is rejected; if one names a row
+  that destroys, it is at least `destructive`. A word that names a row is also read as
+  the start of a possible inner command.
+- **B.** Code the gate cannot read is at least `destructive`, reason "runs code the gate
+  cannot read": stdin read as code that is a pipe, a file or unknown; text that is not a
+  literal, or in a grammar the gate does not parse, or that the parser cannot fully read;
+  a script file. Literal text in a grammar the gate parses is classed again through the
+  whole procedure of §1 (characters, parse, rejections, every rule), with one work budget
+  for the whole top-level command.
+- **C.** A gate failure (an unhandled node, the work budget, a missing or invalid command
+  table, an internal error) is `privileged`, rejected, reason "could not class".
+
+The engine MUST hold these seven rules for every row:
+
+1. **Coverage.** Every binary on the target that elevates, or that runs a shell or an
+   interpreter, has a row, including every other name for the same file (`sudo-rs`,
+   `sudo.ws`, `sudoedit-rs`, `su-rs`). The checked-in `targets/<id>/inventory.toml`
+   (setuid, setgid and file-capability binaries, their aliases, `/etc/shells`, the
+   binaries of the `shells` and `interpreters` package sections, `python3*`, `perl*`,
+   `busybox`, `gdb`) is the list this is checked against. It is reviewed like the table.
+2. **No unread argv.** Every row parses its argv. An option the row does not declare, or a
+   non-literal word where an option may stand, is at least `destructive`, "option the gate
+   does not know"; on a row that elevates it is also rejected. A row MAY describe only some
+   subcommands; the others get the row's stated default, at least `mutating`. Every mode
+   that runs a command, a script or stdin declares what it does when no command is given
+   and when stdin is the terminal (`chroot` and `unshare` start a shell; `bash -s` and
+   `bash /dev/stdin` read the terminal). The one exception is class 7 below.
+3. **Grammar.** Every text field names its grammar: `bash` (and `sh`, read with the same
+   parser), `env-split` (uutils env 0.10.0 `-S`; `\_` outside quotes separates words),
+   `arithmetic`, `prompt` (the expansions bash runs in a prompt string or word list), or a
+   grammar the gate does not parse (`python`, `perl`, `tcl`, `js`, `gdb`, `tmux`,
+   `screen`, …), which is decision B. Text the gate's parser cannot fully translate
+   (`${VAR}` in `env -S`) is decision B. In arithmetic text (the values of `declare -i`,
+   `let`, and every subscript of a name operand of `unset`, `test -v`, `printf -v`,
+   `read`, `mapfile`, `getopts`, `wait -p`, `declare`), any `$`, backquote or `$(` is
+   decision B and is classed as bash text too; a name assigned inside the expression
+   counts as a variable write (rule 5).
+4. **Run-time command words.** Every word that can reach command position when the command
+   runs goes through decision A: the words after `-c` text or a script (`$0`, `$1`, …),
+   the arguments around `{}` in `find -exec` and `xargs`. A non-literal inner command word
+   is decision B.
+5. **Protected variables.** Every field that names a variable is marked. Changing,
+   exporting or pointing a nameref at `SUDO_PROMPT` or `SUDO_ASKPASS` is rejected, with or
+   without `=`; the same on a §3 name is at least `session-altering`.
+6. **Stored code.** Text kept to run later is classed as if it ran now, in its own
+   grammar: `trap` text, `alias` values, `complete -C`, the command part of `bind -x`,
+   `mapfile -C`, and the values of `PROMPT_COMMAND` (scalar or array) and `PS0`–`PS4`,
+   however they are set. A value set from something the gate cannot see (`read`,
+   `printf -v`) is decision B. A file run later (`BASH_ENV`, `ENV`, `enable -f`) is
+   decision B.
+7. **Text runners and the accepted gap.** Every text runner on the target has a row
+   (`busybox`, whose applet is an inner command; `python3`, `perl`, `tclsh`, `gjs`,
+   `gdb`, `screen`), and so do runners named for other hosts (`sg`, `newgrp`, `at`,
+   `batch`, `zsh`, `tmux`, `x-terminal-emulator`). One gap is accepted: an unknown
+   command that takes command text as one quoted word (`foo 'sudo -i'`) stays `mutating`,
+   "not allowlisted", because decision A does not split quoted words; so do the
+   code-running options of commands left at the floor (`git -c core.pager=sh log`,
+   `tar --to-command=sh`, `ssh -o ProxyCommand=sh`; rows 8, 10, 11, 27). They go to the
+   confirm prompt. In the table, git's `-c`, `--config-env` and `--exec-path` carry the
+   `class-7-gap` marker; the loader refuses that marker on any other option.
 
